#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105367 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ee39554-fd77-5e90-baa8-f9320ffc3398" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Continuous Random Variables Given in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "e7c527c9-2dc1-50b2-bbf6-10c1a9d122dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-10837" :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The number of tickets sold for a randomly selected baseball game in a certain stadium\n\n- The length of a randomly selected baseball field.\n\n- The weight, in grams, of a slice cut from a cake weighing $1\\,\\text{kg}$" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,\\ldots,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0, \\infty).$\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0, 1\\,000].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105368 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "295d2025-c958-55e3-a194-5cdb271a510c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10838" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/26d09b87ebbabe4c1e1dcd8d5442b1d3efce216d7f404808a3777b8ffa3304ff.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}({x}^{2}+1),\\, & -1<x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^1 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x = 1$\n\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{3}{8}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}([\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}+1]-[\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}+(-1)]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}([\\frac{1}{3}+1]-[-\\frac{1}{3}-1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(\\frac{4}{3}-(-\\frac{4}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105377 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "32131e34-3141-51b3-8d26-6cee481f168c" :question/math-academy-id "e-11037" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}+k{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} (2x^3+kx^2) \\, \\textrm dx = 1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{3}+k{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{k{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{k}{3}]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac k3+\\dfrac{1}{2} = 1 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad k=\\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ee39554-fd77-5e90-baa8-f9320ffc3398" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Continuous Random Variables Given in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172da751-b7b1-5993-92ce-0b3a89fc22b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305034} {:answer/id #uuid "cba6ca18-28a9-5486-94cd-df83dc6c91fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305035} {:answer/id #uuid "c5612fe7-40d4-514a-8685-6d64f1a2fd2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305036} {:answer/id #uuid "790cf3d3-633d-5625-b997-6c1db9d21bc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305037} {:answer/id #uuid "fe2bbd98-032e-589e-acc1-f778f65eceac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "790cf3d3-633d-5625-b997-6c1db9d21bc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305037} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc281f46-91de-5d1b-9764-36cdc503c98f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78edb6bd-ed48-54d3-90d2-5d11cd8b4ff4" :question/math-academy-id "q-126053" :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The volume, measured in cubic centimeters, of water in a cup of coffee\n- The average length, measured in millimeters, of hairs on a horse\n- The number of hairs on a cat" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,…,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09fc0563-1988-57f7-bb4c-7d963fcceb97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305041} {:answer/id #uuid "a089c314-9a63-5f70-9807-f86b76748344" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305042} {:answer/id #uuid "57a88cc7-240e-5e66-af6b-5db9a3880227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305043} {:answer/id #uuid "771dcc0d-7986-5c88-8d66-a61d9bd57629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305044} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ae8ded-5727-59c9-9a75-04864edb3a09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09fc0563-1988-57f7-bb4c-7d963fcceb97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305041} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5416699-4477-5a8c-886f-c7fd7a060bd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c694066-1106-59d2-ad9b-3f01367d7aea" :question/math-academy-id "q-158419" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/4acac98ef7276cb1e90557ca161666a33ccf0a7627b9ebfcbbe44508b9e5f5bb.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{1}^{3}}{2}]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{0}^{3}}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305046 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "860921e1-d82f-53f2-b046-a9a263c6f79a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305048} {:answer/id #uuid "e8382a17-a8c1-55e1-9b02-d05db9bad780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305049} {:answer/id #uuid "61f7121c-1b73-5779-8250-5b63b3720bf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305050} {:answer/id #uuid "d059a839-1041-59dd-ad47-4215fc8aed35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305051} {:answer/id #uuid "d6769fff-4e88-5ef2-b3b5-78cf8b1ef366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "860921e1-d82f-53f2-b046-a9a263c6f79a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305048} :answer-field/id #uuid "670547b6-a516-535f-860a-d36c62571cc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43c04f14-73dd-5b32-800a-3923c974ccbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-158424" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/819fa17f44f669746ba0c82d2d986f1cd5955a1d7d8144eda6922c0cb7813c9c.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{15}{x}^{3},\\, & 1\\le x<2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{4}{15}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{15}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{4}}{15}]-[\\frac{{1}^{4}}{15}] \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51366a49-fd12-5786-8f7d-f07bc638c401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305055} {:answer/id #uuid "997c191b-7355-5162-8345-97f79fb7d478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305056} {:answer/id #uuid "437df922-4149-5cc6-94d7-2f7829f9639e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305057} {:answer/id #uuid "40f5ebcd-03b2-513b-839f-8c5b12075845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305058} {:answer/id #uuid "5718d1cc-559e-5b30-ac50-6efe49b4568a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40f5ebcd-03b2-513b-839f-8c5b12075845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305058} :answer-field/id #uuid "84690231-57c0-5dab-af90-fa45238c5de9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3503a51d-9484-5e3d-a810-963c28370c44" :question/math-academy-id "q-158427" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/ae6d25fae4127a74b38bcb9c0de8f44705dca81353c89557047e5d35a3465c5b.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3985c5f9-38b1-5a26-a672-4b0618fdaf22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305062} {:answer/id #uuid "411adbc2-34c2-53d3-9ced-593f45056934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305063} {:answer/id #uuid "3830ba36-c0e0-5601-bb05-fdc5a2ac7555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305064} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7be087-5473-5a92-acfe-7787c1468293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305065} {:answer/id #uuid "87219226-24fe-5075-8ae3-8b1a6ac6399b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87219226-24fe-5075-8ae3-8b1a6ac6399b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305066} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0ec7e5f-eb5b-500c-a251-bbe0c67c714b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "135e16d9-b89b-5e2d-b1ba-1746dcd48867" :question/math-academy-id "q-158428" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/6e540dc9309f2a5d1753760b4050c049e380705914d7289b47319de0b0696a9a.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{3}-1),\\, & \\frac{1}{2}<x\\le \\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. $f(x)$ is negative for some value of $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}4({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-x]}_{1/2}^{3/2} \\\\  & =4([\\frac{81}{64}-\\frac{3}{2}]-[\\frac{1}{64}-\\frac{1}{2}]) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{80}{64}-1) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{5}{4}-1) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement I is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abbf513f-2d0b-5290-8b2f-73bb25611fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305069} {:answer/id #uuid "308e70fd-a105-5b0f-8084-0512150f6125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305070} {:answer/id #uuid "9a403c2a-ec63-5482-b350-ff4d598eeadd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305071} {:answer/id #uuid "d3464921-0493-55fb-b4e0-208e05b073dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305072} {:answer/id #uuid "c4bfc7a6-21db-5cac-b811-1eb346bf3f22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3464921-0493-55fb-b4e0-208e05b073dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305072} :answer-field/id #uuid "93f7fbea-2b76-5d32-969a-94f326a9b2b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c7c7a03-37ed-5514-b67b-267ee6ad6e74" :question/math-academy-id "q-158429" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/0c30c392223857c77cfc8619f1fa0d4b7fca20af618bdd424570cc3ed712f38b.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x-1,\\, & 1<x\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}-x]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[9-3]-[1-1] \\\\  & =6-0 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21d558fb-bc8b-5c31-8b2d-3ed9c9ed3dfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305076} {:answer/id #uuid "98ead842-0a26-5dee-82a9-0d4dddca57f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305077} {:answer/id #uuid "f464b124-7687-5c0e-948d-58fa92e3b3d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305078} {:answer/id #uuid "995c4336-6369-579b-be02-56c49c022379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305079} {:answer/id #uuid "673cb1d9-fe16-5dea-b869-52dae83e5415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f464b124-7687-5c0e-948d-58fa92e3b3d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305078} :answer-field/id #uuid "f22139ef-bdce-55c1-946a-29a4e20f6902" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d511a398-3a34-5603-8657-9ec5bfdc0692" :question/math-academy-id "q-158442" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}k(x+1),\\, & 2<x<3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{3}k(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}k(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =k{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x]}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =k([\\frac{9}{2}+3]-[\\frac{4}{2}+2]) \\\\  & =k(\\frac{9}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{2}k=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c33f874-f544-539d-b546-5bbe8d5ccf1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305083} {:answer/id #uuid "1aeaae26-77d5-53e3-a502-e45359f074ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305084} {:answer/id #uuid "04f93f28-7377-5415-b029-9acacd84ccc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305085} {:answer/id #uuid "fbef4d6c-9b31-5339-a7c6-524e820d0210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305086} {:answer/id #uuid "e6475985-8816-54e0-87cd-a18ff7c2079a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c33f874-f544-539d-b546-5bbe8d5ccf1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305083} :answer-field/id #uuid "4376d4a2-a801-5b88-bf49-651fd4b633ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e47ab03-7cd8-5cb5-8a46-6e9648e291bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-158446" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}k-x,\\, & -1<x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(k-x)\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(k-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[kx-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-[-k-\\frac{1}{2}] \\\\  & =k+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk+\\frac{1}{2}=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b2eaee4-b5bc-5e8b-810c-e8d157ffd3bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305090} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3b9c66-3a41-5161-80eb-07798c4cd034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305091} {:answer/id #uuid "2e0eb847-8d62-591e-8b45-6ab03a14bc41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305092} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0a7ba7-ed75-5374-aa83-b7ecf6165e16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305093} {:answer/id #uuid "9d062a76-0166-5376-b966-5c2b21f76c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d062a76-0166-5376-b966-5c2b21f76c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305094} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a332853-7bb0-59d4-a686-56e556a86931" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "87d23167-a7f7-5483-8432-8ac06af4b428" :question/math-academy-id "q-158447" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k},\\, & 1<x<4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{k}{\\int }_{1}^{4}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}([\\frac{64}{3}+4]-[\\frac{1}{3}+1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}(21+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{k}=1\\,⟹\\,k=24.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ee39554-fd77-5e90-baa8-f9320ffc3398" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Continuous Random Variables Given in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd1cad6f-54b0-59f7-a27c-07502506dcdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105246} {:answer/id #uuid "d41320f0-6d5f-54a0-9a41-0e8cdd683431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105247} {:answer/id #uuid "e06d0fa1-e4cf-5240-be12-319ced372bcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105248} {:answer/id #uuid "1d772218-2091-5cc8-9c3d-eb87302490bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105249} {:answer/id #uuid "2006a334-d98b-5871-bbd2-4845c64d439d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d41320f0-6d5f-54a0-9a41-0e8cdd683431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105247} :answer-field/id #uuid "cebe3bcd-dd4e-51f7-be2d-f81cdcb8aa80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70ebefad-2f9e-5da5-8283-6d6972fbe689" :question/math-academy-id "q-86911" :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The percent concentration of fruit concentrate in a randomly selected fruit drink\n- The number of times a coin is flipped until it lands on tails\n- The number of days in a particular week" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,100]$ (since the concentration can be anywhere between $0%$ and $100%$).\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $1,2,3,…,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a continuous random variable. The number of days in a particular week is not the outcome of a trial or experiment. (By definition, there are $7$ days in a week.)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ee39554-fd77-5e90-baa8-f9320ffc3398" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Continuous Random Variables Given in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "264e7fa7-0876-5b2a-bf43-93493d04695e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305097} {:answer/id #uuid "73eff073-4cdd-5c1a-b1b4-689135c3d34b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305098} {:answer/id #uuid "8411ab5e-3a18-586e-9c42-0b14cb5d1680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305099} {:answer/id #uuid "0fcf5f62-da23-59aa-b255-3ce3a003ed2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305100} {:answer/id #uuid "9743560f-1331-5de2-afbf-5d1839dc4047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8411ab5e-3a18-586e-9c42-0b14cb5d1680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305099} :answer-field/id #uuid "756bafd6-1752-5294-a67f-41904811eb76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4e0ce25-445d-58ee-9787-78746dffa9e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-87153" :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The amount of water per month consumed by a randomly selected person\n- The number of pages in a particular book\n- The amount of time that a randomly selected person spends waiting for a particular bus" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a continuous random variable. The number of pages in a particular book is not the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ee39554-fd77-5e90-baa8-f9320ffc3398" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Continuous Random Variables Given in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3f8be16-82d0-514f-bbd6-ff667c3c5eeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305104} {:answer/id #uuid "ed578d21-e7bc-53f7-9966-bc58c20b1c26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305105} {:answer/id #uuid "9a7bc289-8212-5870-a4bb-d6e4f1287766" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305106} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4ed27d-8f1e-5a94-ae15-0d8ad8cc6f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305107} {:answer/id #uuid "464fb7ed-f856-5245-9633-4bbf6e183eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "464fb7ed-f856-5245-9633-4bbf6e183eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305108} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcb53937-43f9-50c6-bc3e-cf9dcd50432a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "206f3fcf-ad34-5fea-b36b-6dd22be1a9ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-87155" :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The score when a roulette wheel is spun at random\n- The amount of water consumed by a randomly selected person on a particular day, measured in liters\n- The number of students in a particular class on a randomly selected day" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible outcomes do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,…,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ee39554-fd77-5e90-baa8-f9320ffc3398" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Continuous Random Variables Given in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f96abd4-1370-585a-a52b-3f9601898726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305111} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf9c9af-a111-59cb-83ca-9fffd32ae6a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305112} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2377dc-878d-5277-996f-e7edfffaf2bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305113} {:answer/id #uuid "54c2c62d-369e-591b-873b-0fd358282f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305114} {:answer/id #uuid "56cb22d8-667b-5a71-b823-aea3823d1f8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cf9c9af-a111-59cb-83ca-9fffd32ae6a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305112} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5fe2173-d9b4-5f3b-b6cd-ba715b63e8f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e3a4770-f26a-5c48-b3f0-e6bca8bc6054" :question/math-academy-id "q-87160" :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The volume of milk produced by a particular cow on a randomly selected day, measured in liters\n- The outcome of rolling a $6$-sided die\n- The distance that a randomly selected runner can run in $30$ minutes" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $1,2,3,…,6,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "455d74f5-6fac-51b6-b06e-3d78221c1199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305118} {:answer/id #uuid "7c168dc6-a35b-59b3-9c1b-fed3f4c924f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305119} {:answer/id #uuid "f05e5822-5d83-57d9-a4fc-4f9d0ae101bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305120} {:answer/id #uuid "344b2fad-b542-57cc-bd95-96f22cff219b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305121} {:answer/id #uuid "728506e6-f2bf-5b99-a467-89061410f923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "728506e6-f2bf-5b99-a467-89061410f923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305122} :answer-field/id #uuid "25fd1f61-d76e-5824-842e-ab1a3a1e9d5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e88718f2-773a-5bee-bcb1-ba3ae7c0536e" :question/math-academy-id "q-87236" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{{x}^{3}},\\, & 3<x<6, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{k}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{k}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}k{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{k}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{72}+\\frac{k}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{24}=1\\,⟹\\,k=24.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91de6dfc-3550-5227-826f-617909d90dc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105348} {:answer/id #uuid "becf5a88-f447-5bbc-9a00-579a74ecfe2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105349} {:answer/id #uuid "e6038154-3664-5600-a7d9-77e6082263e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105350} {:answer/id #uuid "0d5fddf9-5def-5d25-b2b2-98d44685cfd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105351} {:answer/id #uuid "450ea2f9-252f-5675-b796-03caa076d7ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d5fddf9-5def-5d25-b2b2-98d44685cfd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105351} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b3d8b68-2035-520d-82f9-f67ea75460d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57ead9aa-937c-57a1-8fa5-6d07fce39ffb" :question/math-academy-id "q-98912" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}},\\, & 3\\le x\\le 9, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{9}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{9}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{9}k{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{k}{x}]}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{9}+\\frac{k}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2k}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2k}{9}=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$"}]}
